      "solution": "![](images/67/67ef97be4b21a74feaae15f3ff30d5077fc1316874df8de79155316a621e4487.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $N$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n\n- $Z$ represents the set of **integers**.\n\n- $Q$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n\n- $R$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n\n- $C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following:\n\n- First, we note that $\\sqrt{-4}=2\\text{i}$ is a complex number. Therefore, we can write\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{-4}\\in C.\n$$\n\n- Since the imaginary part of $\\sqrt{-4}$ is nonzero, it does *not* belong to $N,Z,Q,$ or $R.$ We can write\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{-4}\\notin N,\\,\\sqrt{-4}\\notin Z,\\,\\sqrt{-4}\\notin Q,\\,\\sqrt{-4}\\notin R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\""
    },
